 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Regulated current",
   "expr": "ITRIP = VTRIP / RISEN = 0.35 V / RISEN",
   "where": {
    "ITRIP": "output current at which chopping starts, A",
    "VTRIP": "ISEN trip voltage, 0.35 V typ",
    "RISEN": "sense resistor from ISEN to ground, Ω"
   },
   "note": "With RISEN = 0.16 Ω the current is held at 2.2 A whatever the load torque.",
   "page": 9
  },
  {
   "name": "Bridge conduction loss",
   "expr": "PD = I² × (RDS(on)Source + RDS(on)Sink)",
   "where": {
    "PD": "conduction loss of the sourcing and sinking FET pair, W",
    "I": "output current, A",
    "RDS(on)Source": "on resistance of the high-side (sourcing) FET",
    "RDS(on)Sink": "on resistance of the low-side (sinking) FET"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 12
  },
  {
   "name": "Sense resistor dissipation",
   "expr": "PR = IRMS² × R",
   "where": {
    "PR": "power in the sense resistor, W",
    "IRMS": "RMS motor current, A",
    "R": "sense resistance, Ω"
   },
   "note": "3 A peak, 1.5 A RMS through 0.2 Ω gives 1.5 A² × 0.2 Ω = 0.45 W.",
   "page": 12
  },
  {
   "name": "Device dissipation",
   "expr": "Power ≈ IRMS² × (High-side RDS(ON) + Low-side RDS(ON))",
   "where": {
    "IRMS": "RMS motor current, A",
    "High-side RDS(ON)": "high-side FET on resistance at the operating temperature",
    "Low-side RDS(ON)": "low-side FET on resistance at the operating temperature"
   },
   "note": "At 58 °C ambient the two resistances add to about 0.72 Ω; 0.8 A RMS then gives 0.8 A² × 0.72 Ω = 0.46 W.",
   "page": 16
  },
  {
   "name": "Junction temperature",
   "expr": "TJ = TA + (PD × RθJA)",
   "where": {
    "TJ": "junction temperature, °C",
    "TA": "ambient temperature, °C",
    "PD": "device dissipation, W",
    "RθJA": "effective junction-to-ambient thermal resistance, °C/W"
   },
   "note": "58 °C + (0.46 W × 48 °C/W) = 80 °C, with an effective RθJA of 48 °C/W on the example board.",
   "page": 16
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Overview",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "Four N-channel FETs form the bridge, 565 mΩ typ for one high-side plus one low-side switch. VM is the only supply: it powers the logic and biases the motor, and an internal charge pump lifts it to drive the high-side gates fully on. Speed can be set by PWM on the inputs at 0 to 100 kHz."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bridge control and PWM",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Steady levels on IN1 and IN2 give 100 % duty drive; PWM on them varies speed. Alternating between drive and brake usually gives the best results: for forward at half speed, IN1 stays 1 while IN2 toggles between 0 (drive) and 1 (brake). Coast (both inputs 0) gives fast decay instead. IN1 and IN2 may be driven before VM is present."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sleep",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Both inputs low for tSLEEP (1 ms typ) puts the chip to sleep, outputs High-Z, drawing IVMSLEEP. Powering up with both inputs low enters sleep at once. Once IN1 or IN2 has been high for 5 µs or more, the bridge works tON (50 µs) later."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current regulation",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "When the voltage on ISEN reaches VTRIP the chip turns both low-side FETs on for tOFF (25 µs typ), a slow-decay period, then returns to the state set by the inputs. A blanking time tBLANK follows each turn-on. How long the drive phase lasts before the next trip depends on VM, the motor's back-EMF and its inductance."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Dead time",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Each output transition from high to low or low to high has tDEAD inserted with the output High-Z to stop shoot-through. During it, current flowing out of the pin holds it one body-diode drop below ground; current flowing in holds it one diode drop above VM."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Protection",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Any fault turns off every bridge FET and drives nFAULT low; nFAULT goes high-impedance again once the fault is gone. Undervoltage: VM below VUVLO; running resumes when VM climbs back over the threshold. Overcurrent: current above IOCP for longer than tOCP stops the bridge for tRETRY, after which it follows the inputs again, repeating while the overload lasts. Thermal: the die over its limit stops the bridge until it has cooled by the hysteresis."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Modes of use",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "PWM with regulation: ITRIP set above running current, high enough for the wanted spin-up time and low enough to cap current; one input carries PWM, the other is static, with brake and slow decay in the off-time. PWM without regulation: ISEN goes straight to the ground plane, allowing up to 3.6 A for some hundreds of milliseconds depending on the board and ambient; above 3.6 A OCP or TSD may act and the bridge rests about 3 ms (tRETRY) before running again. Static inputs with regulation: 100 % duty, ITRIP limiting current, speed and torque. VM control: motor speed changed by varying VM."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Motor voltage",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "VM follows the motor's rating and the wanted speed. For the same duty cycle a higher VM turns a brushed motor faster and makes winding current change faster."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Drive current",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The chip has been measured carrying 2 A RMS at 25 °C on standard FR-4 boards. The RMS limit moves with board design, ambient temperature and PWM frequency; switching at 200 kHz rather than 20 kHz typically adds 20 % to the heat lost."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Sense resistor",
   "page": 12,
   "text": "The ISEN resistor should be a surface-mount, low-inductance part with enough power rating, close to the chip. Allow for its power derating at high ambient and for heat from nearby parts, and check its actual temperature in the finished product. Several ordinary resistors in parallel from the sense node to ground can share the current and heat in place of one power resistor."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bulk capacitance",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "The local bulk capacitor needed depends on the motor system's peak current, the supply's own capacitance and current capability, wiring inductance to the supply, the ripple allowed, the motor type and the braking method. Wire inductance limits how fast the supply can follow load steps, so too little bulk capacitance lets VM move; enough keeps it steady. The value is confirmed by testing the system. The capacitor's voltage rating should exceed the operating voltage with margin for energy the motor returns to the supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Put the bulk capacitor so the high-current loop through the chip is short, with wide copper and many vias between layers. Small capacitors are ceramic and sit at the pins. The outputs get wide traces. Solder the thermal pad to the top-layer ground plane and stitch it with many vias down to a broad ground plane on the bottom; big planes and many vias carry off the I² × RDS(on) heat."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "Above roughly 175 °C die temperature the chip shuts down until it has cooled past the hysteresis. Reaching TSD points to too much dissipation, too little heatsinking or too hot an ambient. Dissipation is mostly I² × RDS(on) in the output FETs; start-up current is far above running current and its size and duration count too. RDS(on) rises with temperature, so dissipation grows as the chip warms. The PowerPAD must be thermally joined to board copper: by vias to an internal ground plane, or on a board without planes by copper on either side, with thermal vias when the copper is on the far side. More copper area lets more power be dissipated."
  }
 ],
